  "title": "Microsoft dependency injection registrations lifetimes scopes disposal and keyed services",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "The built-in container maps service types to implementations or factories. Constructors receive dependencies, while the container controls construction, lifetime caching, scopes, and disposal for instances it creates.",
    "Transient creates an instance per resolution, scoped creates one per scope (normally an HTTP request), and singleton shares one instance for the provider lifetime. A lifetime controls reuse and disposal; it does not make the service thread-safe.",
    "A singleton must not capture scoped state. Background services should create scopes for work that needs scoped services. Resolving disposable transients from the root provider can retain them until shutdown.",
    "Multiple registrations are ordered: a single-service resolution returns the last matching registration, while IEnumerable<T> receives all registrations. Keyed services add an explicit key dimension.",
    "The built-in provider is intentionally simple. Avoid building a second provider during registration, resolving arbitrary services through IServiceProvider inside business logic, or treating service registration as storage for request data."
  ],
